div1.p:
"cf." (French, "confer") as "compare" or "for comparison";

"ed." as "edition";

"e.g." (Latin, "exempli gratia") as "for example" or "for
instance";

"i.e." (Latin, "id est") as "that is";

"lit." as "literal" or "literally";

"LXX" and "Sept." as Septuagint;

"MSS." as "manuscripts,"

"N.B." (Latin, Nota Bene) as "Note";

"q.d." (Latin, quasi dicat) as "As if he should say";

"viz.," (Latin, videlicet) as "namely."

div.p:
13, 14. And Cain said … My punishment is
greater than I can bear—What an overwhelming sense of misery;
but no sign of penitence, nor cry for pardon.

4. a tower whose top may reach unto
heaven—a common figurative expression for great height (De 1:28;
9:1-6).

honey—which some think was
dibs, a syrup made from ripe dates [Bochart]; but others, the honey of Hebron, which is
still valued as far superior to that of Egypt;

21. The Lord make thee a curse,
&c.—a usual form of imprecation (Isa 65:15;
Jer 29:22).

8. A land of wheat, and barley—These
cereal fruits were specially promised to the Israelites in the event of
their faithful allegiance to the covenant of God (Ps 81:16;
147:14). The wheat and barley
were so abundant as to yield sixty and often an hundredfold (Ge 26:12;
Mt 13:8).

12. A land which the Lord thy God careth
for—that is, watering it, as it were, with His own hands,
without human aid or mechanical means.

thou shalt do not work with the firstling of thy
bullock—that is, the second firstlings (see De 12:17, 18;
14:23).

En-shemesh—"the fountain of the sun";
"either the present well of the apostle, below Bethany, on the road to
Jericho, or the fountain near to St. Saba" [Robinson].

let him accept an offering—that is,
let us conjointly offer a sacrifice for appeasing His wrath against
us.

and the Amalekites—Part of the
district occupied by them lay on the south of the land of Israel (Jud 5:14;
12:15).

Heman—(1Ch 15:17-19)—the chief of the temple musicians
and the king's seers (1Ch 25:5);
the other two are not known.

10, 11. I will bring evil upon the house of
Jeroboam—Strong expressions are here used to indicate the
utter extirpation of his house;

9-13. Tou—or Toi—whose dominions
border on those of Hadadezer. (See on 2Sa 8:9-12;
1Ki 11:15).

they came to Jerusalem—The time chosen
for the grand discovery was, probably, one of the annual festivals,
when there was a general concourse of the nation at the capital.

7. Maaseiah the king's son—the sons of
Ahaz being too young to take part in a battle, this individual must
have been a younger son of the late King Jotham;

a little—literally, "a whisper";
implying the still silence around, and that more was conveyed than
articulate words could utter (Job 26:14; 2Co 12:4).

20. from morning to evening—unceasingly;
or, better, between the morning and evening of one short day (so Ex
18:14; Isa 38:12).

20. literally, "each had hoped"; namely, that
their companions would find water. The greater had been their hopes the
more bitter now their disappointment;

4. Literally, "When shall be the flight of the
night?" [Gesenius]. Umbreit, not so well, "The night is long extended";
literally, "measured out" (so Margin).

6. (Isa 38:12). Every day like the weaver's shuttle
leaves a thread behind; and each shall wear, as he weaves. But Job's
thought is that his days must swiftly be cut off as a web;

21. Till—literally, "to the point that";
God's blessing on thee, when repentant, will go on increasing to the
point that, or until, &c.

30. snow water—thought to be more
cleansing than common water, owing to the whiteness of snow (Ps 51:7;
Isa 1:18).

dig—namely, wells; the chief necessity
in the East. Better, "though now ashamed (Ro 5:5, opposed to the previous 'hope'), thou
shalt then rest safely" [Gesenius];

10. destroyed … on every
side—"Shaken all round, so that I fall in the dust"; image
from a tree uprooted by violent shaking from every side [Umbreit]. The last clause accords with this (Jer 1:10)

mine hope—as to this life (in
opposition to Zophar, Job 11:18);
not as to the world to come (Job 19:25; Job 14:15).

mouth—that is, "calling aloud";
formerly a nod was enough. Now I no longer look for
obedience, I try entreaty.

fire—alluding to Job (Job 1:16;
15:34; 18:15). First is
mentioned destruction by water (Job 22:16); here, by fire (2Pe 3:5-7).

beholdeth not—"turneth not to";
figuratively, for He cannot enjoy his pleasant possessions (Job
20:17; 15:33).

3. armies—angels and stars (Isa
40:26; Jer 33:22; Ge 15:5;
"countless," Da 7:10).

dropped—affected their minds, as the
genial rain does the soil on which it gently drops (Am 7:16;
De 32:2; So 4:11).

16. Rather, "He will lead forth thee
also out of the jaws of a strait" (Ps 18:19;
118:5).

20. Desire—pant for. Job had wished
for death (Job 3:3-9,
&c.).

9. south—literally, "chambers";
connected with the south (Job 9:9). The
whirlwinds are poetically regarded as pent up by God in His southern
chambers, whence He sends them forth (so Job 38:22; Ps 135:7). As to the southern whirlwinds (see
Isa 21:1; Zec 9:14), they drive before them burning sands;
chiefly from February to May.

search—Rather, "the inmost recesses";
literally, "that which is only found by searching," the deep caverns of
ocean.

23. against the time of trouble—the time
when I design to chastise men (Ex 9:18; Jos 10:11; Re 16:21; Isa 28:17; Ps 18:12, 13;
Hag 2:17).

driver—who urges on the tame ass to
work. The wild ass is the symbol of uncontrolled freedom in the East;
even kings have, therefore, added its name to them.

condemn—declare Me unrighteous, in
order that thou mayest be accounted righteous (innocent;
undeservingly afflicted).

28. arrow—literally, "son of the bow";
Oriental imagery (La 3:13;
Margin).

and the uttermost parts of the
earth—(Ps 22:27);
denotes universality.

ordained—founded, or prepared, and
perfected, which occurs in Mt 21:16;
taken from the Septuagint, has the same meaning.

3. lighten mine eyes—dim with weakness,
denoting approaching death (compare 1Sa 14:27-29; Ps 6:7;
38:10).

my darling—literally, "my only one,"
or, "solitary one," as desolate and afflicted (Ps 25:16;
35:17).

oracle—place of speaking (Ex 25:22;
Nu 7:89), where God answered
His people (compare Ps 5:7).

20. the secret of thy presence—or,
covering of Thy countenance; the protection He thus affords;
compare Ps
27:5 for a similar figure;
"dwelling" used there for "presence" here. The idea of security further
presented by the figure of a tent and a fortified city [Ps 31:21].

1-3. The sentiment falls in with Ps 32:11 (compare 1Co 14:15). The instruments (Ps 92:3;
144:9) do not exclude the
voice.

11. False witnesses—literally,
"Witnesses of injustice and cruelty" (compare Ps 11:5;
25:19).

light … from me—utter exhaustion
(Ps 6:7;
13:3).

13, 14. He patiently submits, uttering no
reproaches or replies (Joh 19:9) to
their insulting speeches;

3. under us—that is, His saints;
Israel's temporal victories were types of the spiritual conquests of
the true Church.

19. God reconciled, material sacrifices
will be acceptable (Ps 4:5;
compare Isa 1:11-17).

4. all-devouring—literally,
"swallowing," which utterly destroy (compare Ps 21:9;
35:25).

for it is good—that is, Thy name, and
the whole method or result of its manifestation (Ps 54:6;
69:16).

9. God is for me—or, "on my side" (Ps 118:6;
124:1, 2); hence he is sure
of the repulse of his foes.

trust in the covert,
&c.—make my refuge, in the shadow (compare Ps 17:8;
36:7).

6, 7. the king—himself and his royal
line ending in Christ. Mercy and truth personified, as in Ps 40:11;
57:3.

2. The titles applied to God often occur
(Ps 9:9;
18:2).

9. holdeth … in life—literally,
"putteth our soul in life"; that is, out of danger (Ps 30:3;
49:15).

3-5. Thanks will be rendered for the
blessings of His wise and holy government (compare Isa 2:3, 4;
11:4).

16-18. These earnest terms are often used, and
the address to God, as indifferent or averse, is found in Ps 3:7; 22:24;
27:9, &c.

29. poor and sorrowful—the afflicted
pious, often denoted by such terms (compare Ps 10:17;
12:5).

8. in the hand … a cup …
red—God's wrath often thus represented (compare Isa 51:17;
Jer 25:15).

25. angels' food—literally, "bread of
the mighty" (compare Ps 105:40);
so called, as it came from heaven.

18. We need quickening grace (Ps 71:20;
119:25) to persevere in Thy
right worship (Ge 4:26; Ro 10:11).

out of course—(Compare Margin;
Ps 9:6;
62:2).

crieth out—literally, "sings for joy";
but here, and La 2:19,
expresses an act of sorrow as the corresponding noun (Ps 17:1; 61:2).

in whose heart … the ways—that
is, who knows and loves the way to God's favor (Pr 16:17;
Isa 40:3, 4).

6, 7. This is worthy of our belief, for His
faithfulness (is praised) by the congregation of saints or holy ones;
that is, angels (compare De 33:2; Da 8:13).

12-14. The vigorous growth, longevity,
utility, fragrance, and beauty of these noble trees, set forth the
life, character, and destiny of the pious;

2. Lift up thyself—or, "Arise," both
figures representing God as heretofore indifferent (compare Ps 3:7;
22:16, 20).

4. Join joyfully in His public worship. The
terms are, of course, figurative (compare Ps 84:2;
92:13; Isa 66:23).

28-30. sacrifices of the dead—that is,
of lifeless idols, contrasted with "the living God" (Jer 10:3-10; compare Ps 115:4-7;
1Co 12:2). On the words,

4-7. A graphic picture is given of the
sufferings of those who from distant lands returned to Jerusalem;
or,

8. The opposite blessing is long life (Ps 91:16;
Pr 3:2). The last clause is
quoted as to Judas by Peter (Ac 1:20).

7, 8. which condescension is illustrated as
often in raising the worthy poor and needy to honor (compare 1Sa 2:8;
Ps 44:25).

63. The communion of the saints. Delight in
their company is an evidence of belonging to them (Ps 16:3;
Am 3:3; Mal 3:16).

4. the cords—that is, which fasten the
plough to the ox; and cutting denotes God's arresting the
persecution;

from all his iniquities—or,
"punishments of them" (Ps 40:12,
&c.).

3. there—that is, in Zion, the Church;
the material Zion, blessed with enriching dews, suggests this allusion
the source of the influence enjoyed by the spiritual Zion.

21. out of Zion—(Compare Ps 110:2;
134:3). From the Church, as a
center, His praise is diffused throughout the earth.

2-5. This character of the wicked, and the
devices planned against the pious, correspond to Ps 10:7;
31:13; 58:4, &c.

as those that have been long
dead—deprived of life's comforts (compare Ps 40:15;
88:3-6).

7. The fear of the Lord—the principle of
true piety (compare Pr 2:5;
14:26, 27; Job 28:28; Ps 34:11; 111:10; Ac 9:31).

15, 16. The society of the wicked (way or
path) is dangerous. Avoid the beginnings of sin (Pr 4:14;
Ps 1:1; 119:101).

buckler—or safety, or wisdom, which
procures it (compare Pr 3:21; 8:14; 18:1; Job 6:13;
12:13).

froward things—perversity (Pr 6:14;
23:23), what is opposed to
truth.

4. favour—grace, amiability (Pr 22:11;
Ps 45:2); united with
this,

27. (Compare Pr 4:25). Avoid all by-paths of evil (De 2:27;
17:11). A life of integrity
requires attention to heart, speech, eyes, and conduct.

23. without instruction—literally, "in
want of instruction," having refused it (compare Job 13:18;
Heb 11:24).

I … strength—or, "As for me,
understanding is strength to me," the source of power (Ec 9:16); good judgment gives more efficiency to
actions;

21. Fools not only fail to benefit others, as
do the righteous, but procure their own ruin (compare Pr 10:11, 17;
Ho 4:6).

hath hope—trusteth (Pr 10:2;
11:4; Ps 2:12), implying
assurance of help.

16. trouble—agitation, implying the
anxieties and perplexities attending wealth held by worldlings (Pr 16:18;
1Ti 6:6).

hateth gifts—or, "bribes" (Ex 23:8;
Ps 15:5), and is not
avaricious.

8. One so corrupt as to take a bribe evinces
his high estimate of it by subjection to its influence (Pr 18:16;
19:6).

16. Though wealth cannot buy wisdom for those
who do not love it, yet wisdom procures wealth (Pr 3:16;
14:24).

2. that his heart … itself—that
is, takes pleasure in revealing his folly (Pr 12:23;
15:2).

4. high look—(Compare Margin;
Ps
131:1).

ploughing—better "lamp," a frequent
figure for prosperity (Pr 20:20);
hence joy or delight.

30, 31. Men's best devices and reliances are
vain compared with God's, or without His aid (Pr 19:21;
Ps 20:7; 33:17).

4. humility and the fear of the Lord—are
in apposition; one produces the other. On the results, compare Pr 3:16;
8:18.

7. The influence of wealth sets aside moral
distinctions is implied, and, of course, disapproved (compare Pr 19:6;
21:14, &c.).

2. God's unsearchableness impresses us with
awe (compare Isa 45:15; Ro 11:33). But kings, being finite, should confer
with wise counsellors;

hardeneth his heart—makes himself
insensible to sin, and so will not repent (Pr 14:16;
29:1).

hideth his eyes—as the face (Ps 27:9;
69:17), denotes
inattention.

shall be safe—(Compare Margin;
Pr
18:10).

Who … woman—The question implies
that such are rare, though not entirely wanting (compare Pr 18:22;
19:14).

exercised—that is, disciplined;
literally, "that they may thereby chastise, or humble
themselves."

26. True, literally, in the Jewish theocracy;
and in some measure in all ages (Job 27:16, 17; Pr 13:22;
28:8). Though the retribution
be not so visible and immediate now as then, it is no less real.
Happiness even here is more truly the portion of the godly (Ps 84:11; Mt 5:5; Mr 10:29, 30; Ro 8:28; 1Ti
4:8).

over wise—(Job 11:12;
Ro 12:3, 16), presumptuously
self-sufficient, as if acquainted with the whole of divine truth.

yielding pacifieth—(Pr 15:1). This explains "leave not thy place";
do not in a resisting spirit withdraw from thy post of duty
(Ec 8:3).

7. servants upon horses—the worthless
exalted to dignity (Jer 17:25);
and vice versa (2Sa 15:30).

eight—even to more than seven;
that is, "to many" (so "waters," Ec 11:1), nay, even to very many in need
(Job 5:19; Mic 5:5).

2. Illustrating "the evil days" (Jer 13:16). "Light," "sun," &c., express
prosperity; "darkness," pain and calamity (Isa 13:10;
30:26).

kiss—the token of peace from
the Prince of Peace (Lu 15:20);
"our Peace" (Ps 85:10; Col 1:21; Eph 2:14).

in thee—(Isa 61:10;
Php 4:1, 4). Not in our
spiritual frames (Ps 30:6, 7).

who would instruct me—rather, "thou
wouldest instruct me," namely, how I might best please thee (Isa 11:2, 3; 50:4; Lu 12:12; Joh 14:26;
16:13).

thy dross—not thy sins, but the
sinful persons (Jer 6:29);
"enemies" (Isa 1:24);
degenerate princes (see on Isa 1:22),
intermingled with the elect "remnant" of grace.

22. The high ones (Isa 2:11, 13) on whom the people trust, shall
be "brought low" (Isa 3:2);
therefore "cease from" depending on them, instead of on the Lord (Ps 146:3-5).

ornaments of the legs—the short
stepping-chains from one foot to another, to give a measured gait;
attached to the "tinkling ornaments" (Isa 3:16).

changeable—from a root, "to put off";
not worn commonly; put on and off on special occasions. So,
dress-clothes (Zec 3:4).

wine-press—including the wine-fat;
both hewn, for coolness, out of the rocky undersoil of the
vineyard.

16. For—The deliverance implied in the
name "Immanuel," and the cessation of distress as to food (Isa 7:14, 15), shall last only till the child
grows to know good and evil;

the river—Euphrates swollen in spring
by the melting of the snow of the Armenian mountains (compare Isa 8:6;
Isa 7:20).

Prince of Peace—(See on Isa 9:5; Ge 49:10;
Shiloh, "The Tranquillizer"). Finally (Ho 2:18). Even already He is "our peace" (Lu 2:14;
Eph 2:14).

fear of the Lord—reverential, obedient
fear. The first step towards true "knowledge" (Job 28:28;
Ps 111:10).

rod of … mouth—condemning
sentences which proceed from His mouth against the wicked (Re 1:16;
2:16; 19:15, 21).

faces … flames—"their visages
have the livid hue of flame" [Horsley];
with anguish and indignation.

Isa 14:24-27. Confirmation of
This by the Hereforetold Destruction of the Assyrians under
Sennacherib;

2. the people—of Babylon, primarily. Of
the whole Gentile world ultimately (Isa 49:22; 66:20;
60:9).

5. staff—not the scepter (Ps 2:9), but the staff with which one strikes
others, as he is speaking of more tyrants than one (Isa 9:4;
10:24; 14:29) [Maurer].

to the rushing … that
make—rather, "the roaring … roareth" (compare Isa 8:7;
Jer 6:23).

networks—rather, white cloth
(Es 1:6;
8:16).

fled from far—those who had fled
from distant parts to Jerusalem as a place of safety; rather,
fled afar.

counsels of old—(Isa 42:9;
46:10). Purposes planned long
ago; here, as to the deliverance of His people.

swallow up … in
victory—completely and permanently "abolish" (2Ti 1:10;
Re 20:14; 21:4; compare Ge 2:17;
3:22).

2. Address of the returning people to the
gates of Jerusalem (type of the heavenly city, Heb 12:22); (Ps 24:7, 9; 118:19). Antitypically (Re 22:14;
21:25, 27).

5. lofty city—Babylon; representative of
the stronghold of the foes of God's people in all ages (Isa 25:2, 12;
13:14).

19. In antithesis to Isa 26:14, "They (Israel's foes) shall not live";
"Thy (Jehovah's) dead men (the Jews) shall live," that is, primarily,
be restored, spiritually (Isa 54:1-3), civilly and nationally (Isa 26:15); whereas Thy foes shall not;
ultimately, and in the fullest scope of the prophecy, restored to
life literally (Eze 37:1-14; Da 12:2).

8. In measure—not beyond measure; in
moderation (Job 23:6; Ps 6:1; Jer 10:24; 30:11;
46:28).

all the fruit—This is the whole
benefit designed to be brought about by the chastisement;
namely, the removal of his (Israel's) sin (namely, object of idolatry;
De
9:21; Ho 10:8).

set … on fire—burn them as fuel;
"women" are specified, as probably it was their office to collect fuel
and kindle the fire for cooking.

judgment—The priests had the
administration of the law committed to them (De 17:9;
19:17). It was against the
law for the priests to take wine before entering the tabernacle (Le 10:9;
Eze 44:21).

let … kill sacrifices—rather,
"let the beasts (of another year) go round" [Maurer]; that is, after the completion of a year "I
will distress Ariel."

round about—not fully realized
under Sennacherib, but in the Roman siege (Lu 19:43;
21:20).

9. Stay—rather, "Be astounded";
expressing the stupid and amazed incredulity with which the Jews
received Isaiah's announcement.

19. meek—rather, the afflicted
godly: the idea is, virtuous suffering (Isa 61:1;
Ps 25:9; 37:11) [Barnes].

midst of him—that is, his land. Or
else "His children" are the Gentiles adopted among the Israelites,
his lineal descendants (Ro 9:26; Eph 3:6) [Horsley].

battles of shaking—that is, shock of
battles (Isa 19:16;
compare "sift … sieve," Isa 30:28).

10. Many days and years—rather, "In
little more than a year" [Maurer];
literally, "days upon a year" (so Isa 29:1).

treasure—Not so much material wealth
as piety shall constitute the riches of the nation (Pr 10:22;
15:16).

Lebanon—personified; the allusion may
be to the Assyrian cutting down its choice trees (Isa 14:8;
37:24).

king in … beauty—not as now,
Hezekiah in sackcloth, oppressed by the enemy, but King Messiah (Isa 32:1) "in His beauty" (So 5:10, 16;
Re 4:3).

Haran—more to the west. Abraham
removed to it from Ur (Ge 11:31);
the Carroe of the Romans.

1. Set … house in order—Make
arrangement as to the succession to the throne; for he had then no son;
and as to thy other concerns.

silver … gold—partly obtained
from the Assyrian camp (Isa 33:4);
partly from presents (2Ch 32:23, 27-29).

work—or, "recompense for his work";
rather, "recompense which He gives for work" (Isa 62:11; Re
22:12).

knew not—knew not the lesson of
repentance which the judgment was intended to teach (Isa 5:13;
9:13; Jer 5:3).

7. called by my name—belong to Israel,
whose people, as sons of God, bear the name of their Father (Isa 44:5;
48:1).

spirit—including all spiritual and
temporal gifts, as the parallel, "blessing," proves (Isa 11:2;
32:15).

23. Call to inanimate nature to praise God;
for it also shall share in the coming deliverance from "the bondage of
corruption" (Ro 8:20, 21).

saying—rather, "and that saith";
construed with God, not with Cyrus. God's word is
instantaneously efficient in accomplishing His will.

not know—unawares: which thou dost not
apprehend. Proving the fallacy of thy divinations and astrology (Job 9:5;
Ps 35:8).

17. Thy children—Israel (Isa 49:20,
21; Isa 43:6). Jerome reads, for "Thy children," "Thy builders";
they that destroyed thee shall hasten to build thee.

all flesh shall, &c.—the effect on
the world of God's judgments (Isa 66:15, 16, 18, 19;
Re 15:3, 4).

5. opened … ear—(See on Isa 42:20; Isa 48:8);
that is, hath made me obediently attentive (but Maurer, "hath informed me of my duty"), as a
servant to his master (compare Ps 40:6-8, with Php 2:7; Isa 42:1; 49:3, 6; 52:13;
53:11; Mt 20:28; Lu 22:27).

vanish away—literally, "shall be torn
asunder," as a garment [Maurer];
which accords with the context.

marred—Hebrew, "disfigurement";
abstract for concrete; not only disfigured, but disfigurement
itself.

opened not … mouth—Jer 11:19; and David in Ps 38:13, 14;
39:9, prefiguring Messiah
(Mt 26:63; 27:12, 14; 1Pe 2:23).

justify—treat as if righteous;
forensically; on the ground of His meritorious suffering, not
their righteousness.

12. windows—rather, "battlements";
literally, "suns"; applied to battlements from their radiated
appearance.

for a name … everlasting sign—a
perpetual memorial to the glory of Jehovah (Jer 13:11;
33:9).

slaying … children—as a
sacrifice to Molech, &c. (2Ki 17:31; 2Ch 28:3;
33:6).

fail not—Hebrew, "deceive not";
as streams that disappoint the caravan which had expected to find
water, as formerly, but find it dried up (Job 6:15-17).

his arm—(Isa 40:10;
51:5). Not man's arm,
but His alone (Ps 98:1; 44:3).

ye that … mention …
Lord—Hebrew, "ye that are the Lord's remembrancers";
God's servants who by their prayers "put God in remembrance" of His
promises (Isa 43:26);
we are required to remind God, as if God could, which He cannot,
forget His promises (Ps 119:49; Jer 14:21).

8. sworn by … right hand—His
mighty instrument of accomplishing His will (compare Isa 45:23;
Heb 6:13).

standard—for the dispersed Jews to
rally round, with a view to their return (Isa 49:22;
11:12).

12. The right hand of Moses was but the
instrument; the arm of God was the real mover (Ex 15:6;
14:21).

sounding of … bowels—Thine
emotions of compassion (Isa 16:11; Jer 31:20; 48:36;
Ho 11:8).

so—God will spare the godly "remnant,"
while the ungodly mass of the nation shall be destroyed (Isa 1:9; 6:13; 10:21; 11:11,
12-16).

bring … your brethren—the Jews,
back to the Holy Land (Isa 49:22).
It cannot mean the mere entrance of the Jews into the Christian Church;
for such an entrance would be by faith, not upon "horses,
litters, and mules" [Houbigant].
"Offering" is metaphorical, as in Ro 15:16.

traversing—literally, "enfolding";
making its ways complicated by wandering hither and thither,
lusting after the male. Compare as to the Jews' spiritual lust, Ho 2:6, 7.

36. gaddest—runnest to and fro, now
seeking help from Assyria (2Ch 28:16-21), now from Egypt (Jer 37:7, 8;
Isa 30:3).

yet return—(Jer
3:22; Jer 4:1; Zec 1:3;
compare Eze 16:51, 58, 60). "Nevertheless," &c. (see on Isa 50:1).

27. full end—utter destruction: I will
leave some hope of restoration (Jer 5:10, 18; 30:11;
46:28; compare Le 26:44).

casteth out—causeth to flow;
literally, "causeth to dig," the cause being put for the effect (2Ki
21:16, 24; Isa 57:20).

incense … cane—(Isa 43:24;
60:6). No external services
are accepted by God without obedience of the heart and life (Jer 7:21; Ps 50:7-9; Isa 1:11; Mic 6:6, &c.).

generation of his wrath—the generation
with which He is wroth. So Isa 10:6;
"the people of My wrath."

dwellings cast us out—fulfilling Le 18:28;
20:22. Calvin translates, "The enemy have cast down
our habitations."

know thee … call … on thy
name—Knowledge of God is the beginning of piety;
calling on Him the fruit.

16. swear by my name—(Jer 4:2;
Isa 19:18; 65:16); that is,
confess solemnly the true God.

remember … iniquity—(Ho 8:13;
9:9). Their sin is so great,
God must punish them.

to follow thee—literally, "after
thee"; as an under-pastor following Thee, the Chief Shepherd (Ec 12:11;
1Pe 5:4).

the men—the elders of the people and
of the priests (Jer 19:1;
compare Jer 51:63, 64).

weary with forbearing, and I could
not—"I labored to contain myself, but I could not" (Ac 18:5; compare Jer 23:9;
1Co 9:16, 17).

vessel … no pleasure—(Ps 31:12;
Ho 8:8). The answer to this
is given (Ro 9:20-23; contrast 2Ti 2:21).

righteous Branch—"the Branch of
righteousness" (Jer 33:15);
"The Branch" simply (Zec 3:8; 6:12); "The Branch of the Lord" (Isa 4:2).

course … evil—They (both
prophets and people) rush into wickedness (Jer 23:21;
Isa 59:7).

1. Lord showed me—Am 7:1, 4, 7;
8:1, contains the same
formula, with the addition of "thus" prefixed.

not pull … down … not pluck …
up—only partially fulfilled in the restoration from Babylon;
antitypically and fully to be fulfilled hereafter (Jer 32:41;
33:7).

for they shall return—Repentance,
though not the cause of pardon, is its invariable accompaniment: it is
the effect of God's giving a heart to know Him.

14. serve themselves—(Jer 27:7;
30:8; 34:10). Avail
themselves of their services as slaves.

my first-born—(Ex 4:22;
Ho 11:1; Ro 9:4). So the
elect Church (2Co 6:18; Jas 1:18).

remember—with favor and concern, as in
Ge 8:1;
30:22.

for all that they have done—namely,
all the sins. God will regard His own covenant promise, rather than
their merits.

1. shut up—(Jer 32:2, 3;
2Ti 2:9). Though Jeremiah was
shut up in bondage, the word of God was "not bound."

sacrifice of praise—(Ps 107:22;
116:17). This shall continue
when all other sacrifices shall be at an end.

15. scribe—one of the court secretaries;
often in the East part of the private house of a public officer serves
as a prison.

23. children—(Jer 39:6;
41:10). "wives …
children … thou"; an ascending climax.

Nergalsharezer, Samgarnebo—proper
names formed from those of the idols, Nergal and Nebo (2Ki 17:30;
Isa 46:1).

their wives—The Jews' worldly queens
were great promoters of idolatry (1Ki 11:1-8; 15:13;
16:31).

27. watch over … for evil—(Jer 1:10;
Eze 7:6). The God, whose
providence is ever solicitously watching over His people for good,
shall solicitously, as it were, watch for their hurt. Contrast Jer 31:28;
32:41.

19. he—Nebuchadnezzar, or Nebuzara-dan;
the name would at once suggest itself to the minds of the hearers
(Jer 48:40; 46:18).

shepherd—leader of the Idumeans;
following up the previous image, "a lion"; no Idumean shepherd shall
withstand the lion sent by Jehovah (Job 41:10), or save the Idumean flock.

Merodach—another Babylonian idol;
meaning in Syria "little lord"; from which Merodach-baladan took his
name.

9. gave judgment upon him—as guilty of
rebellion and perjury (Jer 52:3;
compare Eze 23:24).

cast down from heaven unto …
earth—(Mt 11:23);
dashed down from the highest prosperity to the lowest misery.

beauty of Israel—the beautiful temple
(Ps 29:2; 74:7; 96:9, Margin; Isa 60:7;
64:11).

24. (Nu 18:20;
Ps 16:5; 73:26; 119:57; Jer 10:16). To have God for our portion is the one
only foundation of hope.

pine away—literally, "flow out";
referring to the flow of blood. This expression, and "stricken
through," are drawn from death by "the sword."

anger—literally, "face"; it is the
countenance which, by its expression, manifests anger (Ps 34:16). Gesenius translates, "the person of Jehovah";
Jehovah present; Jehovah Himself (Ex 33:14; 2Sa 17:11).

13. young men … grind—The work of
the lowest female slave was laid on young men (Jud 16:21;
Job 31:10).

flash of lightning—rather, as distinct
from "lightning" (Eze 1:13),
"the meteor flash," or sheet lightning [Fairbairn].

9. As … flint—so Messiah the
antitype (Isa 50:7;
compare Jer 1:8, 17).

seek a vision—to find some way of
escape from their difficulties (Isa 26:9). So Zedekiah consulted Jeremiah (Jer 37:17;
38:14).

17. set thy face—put on a bold
countenance, fearlessly to denounce them (Eze 3:8, 9;
Isa 50:7).

11. Love was the spring of God's very
judgments on His people, who were incurable by any other process (Eze 11:20;
37:27).

justified thy sisters—made them appear
almost innocent by comparison with thy guilt (Jer 3:11;
Mt 12:41, 42).

branch—the peculiar title of Messiah
(Zec 3:8; 6:12; Isa 11:1; 4:2; Jer 23:5;
33:15).

from all … transgressions—not as
if believers are perfect; but they sincerely aim at perfection,
so as to be habitually and wilfully on terms with no sin (1Jo 3:6-9):

15. I swore against them (Ps 95:11;
106:26) that I would not
permit the generation that came out of Egypt to enter Canaan.

31. their own way … recompensed upon their
heads—(Eze 9:10; 11:21;
16:43; Pr 1:31; Isa 3:11; Jer 6:19).

24. with chariots—or, "with armaments";
so the Septuagint; "axes" [Maurer]; or, joining it with "wagons," translate,
"with scythe-armed wagons," or "chariots" [Grotius].

13. And I will bring them out from the people,
&c.—(Eze 28:25; 36:24;
37:21, 22; Isa 65:9, 10; Jer 23:3).

24. my servant—implying fitness for
ruling in the name of God, not pursuing a self-chosen course, as other
kings, but acting as the faithful administrator of the will of God;
Messiah realized fully this character (Ps 40:7, 8;
Isa 42:1; 49:3, 6; 53:11; Php 2:7), which David typically and partially
represented (Ac 13:36);
so He is the fittest person to wield the world scepter, abused by all
the world kings (Da 2:34, 35, 44, 45).

my sanctuary—the temple of God;
spiritual in the heart of all true followers of Messiah (2Co 6:16); and, in some literal sense, in the
restored Israel (Eze 40:1-44:31).

eat bread before the Lord—a custom
connected with sacrifices (Ge 31:54; Ex 18:12; 24:11;
1Co 10:18).

30. give … priest the first … that he
may cause the blessing to rest—(Pr 3:9, 10;
Mal 3:10).

removed kings—(Job
12:18; Ps 75:6, 7; Jer 27:5;
compare 1Sa 2:7, 8).

for their sakes that shall make known,
&c.—a Chaldee idiom for, "to the intent that the
interpretation may be made known to the king."

28. In giving some better traits in
Nebuchadnezzar's character, Daniel agrees with Jer 39:11;
42:12.

10. tree—So the Assyrian is compared to
a "cedar" (Eze 31:3;
compare Eze 17:24).

three times a day—(Ps 55:17). The third, sixth, and ninth hour; our
nine, twelve, and three o'clock (Ac 2:15; 10:9; 3:1;
10:30; compare Da 9:21).

eagle's wings—denoting a widespread
and rapidly acquired (Isa 46:11; Jer 4:13; La 4:19;
Hab 1:6) empire (Jer 48:40).

also … against the Prince of
princes—not merely against the Jews (Da 8:11;
11:36).

6. in … end of years—when the
predicted time shall be consummated (Da 11:13, Margin; Da 8:17;
12:13).

even to his fortress—Ptolemy's;
Raphia, a border-fortress of Egypt against incursions by way of Edom
and Arabia-Petræa, near Gaza; here Antiochus was vanquished.

joined to—closely and voluntarily;
identifying themselves with them as a whoremonger becomes one flesh
with the harlot (Nu 25:3; 1Co 6:16, 17).

5. the pride of Israel—wherewith they
reject the warnings of God's prophets (Ho 5:2), and prefer their idols to God (Ho 7:10;
Jer 13:17).

12. great things of … law—(De 4:6, 8; Ps 19:8; 119:18, 72;
147:19, 20). Maurer not so well translates, "the many
things of My law."

for the multitude of thine iniquity,
&c.—Connect these words with, "the days of visitation …
are come"; "the prophet … is mad," being parenthetical.

1. empty—stripped of its fruits [Calvin], (Na 2:2); compelled to pay tribute to Pul (2Ki 15:20). Maurer translates, "A widespreading vine"; so
the Septuagint. Compare Ge 49:22; Ps 80:9-11;
Eze 17:6.

6. It … also—The calf, so far from
saving its worshippers from deportation, itself shall be carried off;
hence "Israel shall be ashamed" of it.

9. Gibeah—(Ho 9:9;
Jud 19:1-20:48). They are
singled out as a specimen of the whole nation.

in a morning—that is, speedily, as
quickly as the dawn is put to flight by the rising sun (Ho 6:4;
13:3; Ps 30:5).

wait on thy God—alone, not on thy
idols. Including all the duties of the first table (Ps 37:3, 5, 7;
40:1).

I am … a green fir—or cypress;
ever green, winter and summer alike; the leaves not falling off in
winter.

bridegroom—ordinarily exempted from
public duties (De 24:5;
compare 1Co 7:5, 29).

roar—as a lion (Jer 25:30;
Am 1:2; 3:8). Compare as to
Jehovah's voice thundering, Ps 18:13; Hab 3:10, 11.

not … pass by … any
more—not forgive them any more (Am 8:2;
Pr 19:11; Mic 7:18).

though they climb up to heaven—though
they ascend the greatest heights (Job 20:6, 7; Jer 51:53;
Ob 4).

3. Carmel—where the forests, and, on the
west side, the caves, furnished hiding-places (Am 1:2;
Jud 6:2; 1Sa 13:6).

be as though they had not been—not a
trace left of their national existence (Job 10:19; Ps 37:36;
Eze 26:21).

3. tread upon the high places of the
earth—He shall destroy the fortified heights (compare De 32:13;
33:29) [Grotius].

her … driven out—all Israel's
outcasts. Called "the Lord's flock" (Jer 13:17; Eze 34:13;
37:21).

hath showed thee—long ago, so that
thou needest not ask the question as if thou hadst never heard (Mic 6:6; compare De 10:12;
30:11-14).

the man of wisdom—As in Pr 13:6, Hebrew, "sin" is used for
"a man of sin," and in Ps 109:4, "prayer" for "a man of prayer";
so here "wisdom" for "the man of wisdom."

17. lick the dust—in abject prostration
as suppliants (Ps 72:9;
compare Isa 49:23; 65:25).

to Jacob … Abraham—Thou shalt
make good to their posterity the promise made to the patriarchs. God's
promises are called "mercy," because they flow slowly from grace;
"truth," because they will be surely performed (Lu 1:72, 73;
1Th 5:24).

curtains—the coverings of their tents;
the shifting habitations of the nomad tribes, which resembled the
modern Bedouins.

4. stretch out mine hand—indicating some
remarkable and unusual work of vengeance (Isa 5:25;
9:12, 17, 21).

my servant—the characteristic title of
Messiah (Isa 42:1; 49:3;
50:10; 52:13; 53:11; Eze 34:23, 24).

as … corners—or "horns" of the
altar, which used to be sprinkled with blood from the bowls (Ex 29:12;
Le 4:18).

feeble … shall be as David—to
the Jew, the highest type of strength and glory on earth (2Sa 17:8;
18:3; Joe 3:10).

former sea—that is, the front,
or east, which Orientalists face in taking the points of the compass;
the Dead Sea.

prove me … herewith—with this;
by doing so. Test Me whether I will keep My promise of blessing you, on
condition of your doing your part (2Ch 31:10).

27. And the rain descended, and the floods came,
and the winds blew, and beat upon that house—struck against
that house;

blockquote.p:
And another also said, Lord, I will follow thee;
but let me first go bid them farewell which are at home at my
house.

div.p:
Mt 9:1-8.
Healing of a Paralytic. ( = Mr 2:1-12;
Lu 5:17-26).

and Judas Iscariot—that is, Judas of
Kerioth, a town of Judah (Jos 15:25);
so called to distinguish him from "Judas the brother of James" (Lu 6:16).

nor the servant above his lord—another
maxim which our Lord repeats in various connections (Lu 6:40;
Joh 13:16; 15:20).

and no man knoweth the Son, but the Father;
neither knoweth any man the Father, save the Son, and he to whomsoever
the Son will—willeth

Mt 13:1-52. Jesus Teaches
by Parables. ( = Mr 4:1-34; Lu 8:4-18;
13:18-20).

31. Another parable put he forth unto them,
saying, The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed, which
a man took, and sowed in his field;

Mt 19:1-12. Final Departure
from Galilee—Divorce. ( =
Mr 10:1-12; Lu 9:51).

33. And he shall set the sheep on his right
hand—the side of honor (1Ki 2:19; Ps 45:9;
110:1, &c.).

Mt 27:34-50. Crucifixion and
Death of the Lord Jesus. ( = Mr 15:25-37;
Lu 23:33-46; Joh 19:18-30).

for he taught them as one that had authority,
and not as the scribes—See on Mt 7:28,
29.

Mr 6:14-29. Herod Thinks
Jesus a Resurrection of the Murdered Baptist—Account of His Death. ( = Mt 14:1-12;
Lu 9:7-9).

heard of him; (for his name was spread abroad);
and he said—"unto his servants" (Mt 14:2), his councillors or court
ministers.

54. And when they were come out of the ship,
straightway they knew him—"immediately they recognized Him";
that is, the people did.

Mr 10:1-12. Final Departure
from Galilee—Divorce. ( =
Mt 19:1-12; Lu 9:51).

28. And one of the scribes—"a lawyer,"
says Matthew (Mt 22:35);
that is, teacher of the law.

Mr 15:21-37. Crucifixion and
Death of the Lord Jesus. ( = Mt 27:32-50;
Lu 23:26-46; Joh 19:17-30).

65. fear—religious awe; under the
impression that God's hand was specially in these events (compare Lu 5:26;
7:16; 8:37).

43. as they returned—If the duties of
life must give place to worship, worship, in its turn, must give place
to them. Jerusalem is good, but Nazareth is good, too;
let him who neglects the one, on pretext of attending to the other,
ponder this scene.

Iturea—to the northeast of Palestine;
so called from Ishmael's son Itur or Jetur (1Ch 1:31), and anciently belonging to the half
tribe of Manasseh.

Mary Magdalene—that is, probably, of
Magdala (on which see Mt 15:39;
see on Mr 8:10).

17. supper-time … all now
ready—pointing undoubtedly to the now ripening preparations
for the great Gospel call. (See on Mt 22:4.)

16. But Jesus—"much displeased,"
says Mark (Mr 10:14);
and invaluable addition.

drawing nigh to the ship—yet as though
He "would have passed by them," Mr 6:48 (compare Lu 24:28; Ge 18:3, 5;
32:24-26).

as I said to the Jews—(Joh 7:34;
8:21). But oh in what a
different sense!

24. foretold of these days—of Messiah;
all pointing to "the time of reformation" (Heb 9:10), though with more or less
distinctness.

which was published throughout all Judea, and
began from Galilee—(See Lu 4:14, 37, 44; 7:17;
9:6; 23:5).

14-17. Simeon—a Hebrew variation
of Simon, as in 2Pe 1:1;
(Greek), the Jewish and family name of Peter.

whose heart the Lord opened—that is,
the Lord Jesus (see Ac 16:15;
and compare Lu 24:45; Mt 11:27).

his life is in him—now restored;
compare Mr
5:39.

is death—not only "ends in" [Alford, &c.], but even now "is"; carrying
death into its bosom, so that such are "dead while they live" (1Ti 5:6;
Eph 2:1, 5) [Philippi].

9. Let love be without
dissimulation—"Let your love be unfeigned" (as in 2Co 6:6;
1Pe 2:22; and see 1Jo 3:18).

24. called—(compare 1Co 1:26). The same class as the "us which are
(being) saved" (1Co 1:18);
the elect, who have obeyed the call; called effectually (Ro 8:28, 30).

testimony of God—"the testimony of
Christ" (1Co 1:6);
therefore Christ is God.

saith he—God speaking by Adam (Ge 2:24;
Mt 19:5). "He which made them
at the beginning said," &c. (Eph 5:31).

5. Defraud … not—namely, of the
conjugal duty "due" (1Co 7:3;
compare the Septuagint, Ex 21:10).

18. Israel after the flesh—the literal,
as distinguished from the spiritual, Israel (Ro 2:29;
4:1; 9:3; Ga 4:29).

a tongue … a revelation—The
oldest manuscripts transpose the order: "revelation … tongue";
"interpretation" properly following "tongue" (1Co 14:13).

some—Gentile reasoners (Ac 17:32;
26:8) who would not believe
it because they did not see "how" it could be (1Co 15:35, 36).

23. The grace, &c.—This is the
salutation meant in 1Co 16:21;
and from which unbelievers (1Co 16:22;
compare 2Jo 10:11)
are excluded [Bengel].

1. workers together—with God (Ac 15:4;
1Co 3:9). Not only as
"ambassadors."

1. cleanse ourselves—This is the
conclusion of the exhortation (2Co 6:1, 14; 1Jo 3:3;
Re 22:11).

who have begun before—"seeing
that ye have begun before" the Macedonian churches;
"a year ago" should be connected with this clause.

3. have I sent—we should say, "I send";
whereas the ancients put it in the past, the time which it would be by
the time that the letter arrived.

of—that is, from the Lord;
Christ, 2Co
12:2.

if I come again, &c.—that is,
whensoever I come again (Ac 20:2). These were probably the very words of
his former threat which he now repeats again.

6. Greek, "From those who," &c. He
meant to complete the sentence with "I derived no special advantage";
but he alters it into "they … added nothing to me."

withdrew—Greek, "began
to withdraw," &c. This implies a gradual drawing back;
"separated," entire severance.

be with your spirit—which, I trust,
will keep down the flesh (1Th 5:23; 2Ti 4:22;
Phm 25).

9. Not of works—This clause stands in
contrast to "by grace," as is confirmed by Ro 4:4, 5;
11:6.

testify in the Lord—in whom (as our
element) we do all things pertaining to the ministry (1Th 4:1 [Alford];
Ro 9:1).

19. for me—a different Greek
preposition from that in Eph 6:18;
translate, therefore, "on my behalf."

Tychicus—an Asiatic, and so a fit
messenger bearing the respective Epistles to Ephesus and Colosse (Ac 20:4;
2Ti 4:12).

7. gain—rather as Greek, "gains";
including all possible advantages of outward status, which he had
heretofore enjoyed.

if ye be dead—Greek, "if ye
died (so as to be freed) from," &c. (compare Ro 6:2;
7:2, 3; Ga 2:19).

23. have—Greek, "are having";
implying the permanent characteristic which these ordinances are
supposed to have.

ye also—like other believers;
answering to "ye also" (Col 3:7) like
other unbelievers formerly.

humbleness of mind—True "lowliness of
mind"; not the mock "humility" of the false teachers (Col 2:23;
Eph 4:2, 32).

a door of utterance—Translate, "a door
for the word." Not as in Eph 6:19,
where power of "utterance" is his petition. Here it is an opportunity
for preaching the word, which would be best afforded by his
release from prison (1Co 16:9; 2Co 2:12; Phm 22;
Re 3:8).

I sent—Paul was the actual sender;
hence the "I" here: Paul, Silas, and Timothy himself had agreed on the
mission already, before Paul went to Athens: hence the "we," (see on 1Th 3:1).

1. Furthermore—Greek, "As to what
remains." Generally used towards the close of his Epistles (Eph 6:10;
Php 4:8).

5. in the lust—Greek, "passion";
which implies that such a one is unconsciously the passive slave
of lust.

unto—rather as Greek, "in";
marking that "holiness" is the element in which our calling has place;
in a sphere of holiness. Saint is another name for
Christian.

watch—for Christ's coming; literally,
"be wakeful." The same Greek occurs in 1Co 15:34;
2Ti 2:26.

chosen you—The Greek, is not
the ordinary word for "elected," implying His eternal selection;
but taken for Himself, implying His having adopted them
in His eternal purpose. It is found in the Septuagint (De 7:7;
10:15).

16. Howbeit—Greek, "But";
contrasting his own conscious sinfulness with God's gracious visitation
of him in mercy.

I commit—as a sacred deposit (1Ti 6:20;
2Ti 2:2) to be laid before
thy hearers.

if … with thanksgiving—Meats,
though pure in themselves, become impure by being received with an
unthankful mind (Ro 14:6; Tit 1:15).

raiment—Greek, "covering";
according to some including a roof to cover us, that is, a
dwelling, as well as clothing.

the affairs of (this) life—"the
businesses of life" [Alford];
mercantile, or other than military.

long-suffering—towards my adversaries,
and the false teachers; towards brethren in bearing their infirmities;
towards the unconverted, and the lapsed when penitent (2Ti 4:2; 2Co 6:6; Ga 5:22; Eph 4:2; Col 3:12).

kept the faith—the Christian faith
committed to me as a believer and an apostle (compare 2Ti 1:14;
Re 2:10; 3:10).

sober—towards one's self;
"discreet"; "self-restrained" [Alford],
(see on 1Ti 2:9).

by—Translate as Greek, "to
exhort in doctrine (instruction) which is sound";
sound doctrine or instruction is the element IN which his exhorting is to have place
… On "sound" (peculiar to the Pastoral Epistles), see 1Ti 1:10;
6:3.

temperate—"self-restrained";
"discreet" [Alford], (Tit 1:8; 1Ti
2:9).

may be ashamed—put to confusion by the
power of truth and innocence (compare Tit 2:5, 10; 1Ti 5:14;
6:1).

transgression—by doing evil;
literally, overstepping its bounds: a positive violation of
it.

from all his works—Hebrew,
Ge 2:2, "from all His work." God's
"work" was one, comprehending, however, many "works."

7. in the days of his flesh—(Heb 2:14;
10:20). Heb 5:7-10 state summarily the subject about
to be handled more fully in the seventh and eighth chapters.

followers—Greek, "imitators";
so in Eph
5:1, Greek; 1Co 11:1.

for that—"seeing that," literally,
"if"; so Ro
5:10.

make—Greek, "perfect";
"consummate." A suitable expression as to the new covenant, which
perfected what the old could not (compare end of Heb 8:9, with end of Heb 8:10).

and their iniquities—not found in
Vulgate, Syriac, Coptic, and one oldest Greek manuscript;
but most oldest manuscripts have the words (compare Heb 10:17).

with a true heart—without hypocrisy;
"in truth, and with a perfect heart"; a heart thoroughly imbued with
"the truth" (Heb 10:26).

and … is—a different
Greek verb from the former "is." Translate, "is eventually";
proves to be; literally, "becomes."

of God—by agency proceeding
from God. The Greek is not "tempted by," but,
"from," implying indirect agency.

disallowed—rejected, reprobated;
referred to also by Christ Himself: also by Paul; compare the kindred
prophecies, Isa 8:14; Lu 2:34.

for conscience toward God—literally,
"consciousness of God": from a conscientious regard to God, more than
to men.

giveth—Greek, "supplieth";
originally said of a choragus, who supplied the chorus
with all necessaries for performing their several parts.

in due time—Wait humbly and patiently
for His own fit time. One oldest manuscript and Vulgate read,
"In the season of visitation," namely, His visitation in mercy.

8. be—Greek, "subsist" that is,
supposing these things to have an actual subsistence in you;
"be" would express the mere matter-of-fact being (Ac 16:20).

damnable—literally, "of destruction";
entailing destruction (Php 3:19) on
all who follow them.

his promise—(Isa 65:17;
66:22). The "we" is not
emphatical as in English Version.

14. that ye … be found of him—"in
His sight" [Alford], at His coming;
plainly implying a personal coming.

whither—Greek, "where";
including not only the destination to which, but the way
whereby.

28. little children—Greek,
"little sons," as in 1Jo 2:12;
believers of every stage and age.

washed … robes … white in the blood
of … Lamb—(Re 1:5; Isa 1:18; Heb 9:14;
1Jo 1:7; compare Isa 61:10;
Zec 3:3-5). Faith applies to
the heart the purifying blood; once for all for justification,
continually throughout the life for sanctification.

Babylon—here first mentioned;
identical with the harlot, the apostate Church; distinct from
the beast, and judged separately.

repented not to give him glory—(Re 9:20). Affliction, if it does not melt,
hardens the sinner. Compare the better result on others, Re 11:13;
14:7; 15:4.

true—Greek, "genuine";
veritable sayings which shall surely be fulfilled, namely, all the
previous revelations.

for—A, B, Vulgate, Syriac,
Coptic, Andreas, and Cyprian omit "for"; which accords with the abrupt
earnestness of the angel's prohibition of an act derogatory to God.
